      answer: 1,
      why: 'Civilisation narratives ran agriculture → settlement → surplus → monuments. Here were megalithic ritual complexes a millennium or more before agriculture appears in the region — suggesting that gathering to build (and feast) may have pulled plant cultivation into being, not the reverse.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Binford\'s "middle range theory" links static archaeological remains to the dynamic behaviour that produced them. What supplies the bridge?',
      choices: [
        'Guesswork checked by peers',
        'Actualistic studies — observing known processes today (experimental flintknapping, butchery marks, ethnoarchaeology among living groups, taphonomy) to learn what which traces look like',
        'Historical documents',
        'Radiocarbon dates',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'You cannot see the past making its deposits, so you observe the present making equivalent ones: knap stone yourself, follow butchers, watch foragers discard. Once "this fracture pattern means X" is calibrated, the frozen record becomes legible.',
    },
  ],
};

