---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/intermediate/archaeology-reading-material-culture">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Anthropology lesson. It assumes the foundational
    <em>Culture, Kin and the Long View</em> lesson — you know what culture means as the
    discipline uses the word — and it pairs naturally with the intermediate
    <em>The Four Fields</em> lesson, where archaeology appears as one of the four. Here
    the field gets its own treatment: the method for reconstructing societies that cannot
    be interviewed.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="choose the bridge from static to dynamic" widget="branch" config={anMiddleRangeBranch} />
  <Widget no="5" caption="order the one-chance procedure" widget="order" config={anExcavationOrder} />
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why context, not the object, is archaeology's unit of evidence;</li>
    <li>read a site through stratigraphy, association and cross-cutting relationships;</li>
    <li>choose between the dating clocks and state each one's material and range;</li>
    <li>trace the pipeline from survey and excavation through analysis to interpretation;</li>
    <li>evaluate the ethical duties — provenance, repatriation, descendant communities — that now govern the field.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Archaeology recovers <strong>the 99% of human history that predates writing</strong> — plus
    the unrecorded everyday of historical eras, the lives of the illiterate, and the
    things nobody thought worth writing down. Its method is a full course in data
    collection under brutal constraints: the data are already damaged, the recording is
    destructive (digging is unrepeatable), and every inference must be built from
    fragments to societies by explicit, checkable logic. This page follows the pipeline
    — fieldwork, data collection, analysis, dating, interpretation, ethics — the same
    skeleton as the ethnographic-methods lesson, adapted to a subject that cannot
    interview its informants.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> archaeologists dig up dinosaurs. Dinosaur fossils belong to palaeontology, a different discipline working on a timescale of millions of years; archaeology studies the human past — at most a few million years of hominin traces, overwhelmingly the last hundred thousand. The confusion is harmless in a film and career-defining in a university application.</p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="context, stratigraphy, the dating clocks, society from stuff" widget="equationStep" config={archCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels in order — context,
    stratigraphy, the dating clocks, the society-from-stuff payoff — and notice how each
    answers a question the previous one raises: where an object sits, how deep time is
    stacked there, which clock can measure it, and what it finally says about the people
    who dropped it. The dating panel names a different clock per material; the rule that
    several clocks must cross-check is the discipline's quality control.
  </p>

  <h2>Fieldwork I: survey — finding what survives</h2>
  <p>
    Before excavation comes the <strong>survey</strong>: walking landscape, mapping
    artefact scatters, test-pitting, and reading the ground from above.
    <strong>Remote sensing</strong> has revolutionised this layer. LiDAR — laser pulses
    flown over jungle that strip away canopy in the digital model — revealed tens of
    thousands of previously unmapped structures in the Maya lowlands (the 2018 PACUNAM
    survey of Guatemala's Petén), with causeways, terraces and fortifications joining
    city to city, and Late Classic population estimates revised upward to millions. A
    landscape emptied by conquest and overgrowth turned out to be one of the densest
    pre-industrial settlement systems known — discovered without lifting a trowel.
    Survey also forces archaeology's statistical discipline: you cannot examine
    everything, so sampling strategy (where you walk, where you dig) becomes part of
    the argument, and "absence of evidence" claims must survive the sampling design
    that produced them.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="guess what LiDAR found without a trowel" widget="estimate" config={anLidarEstimate} />

  <h2>Fieldwork II: excavation — reading layers, destroying them</h2>
  <p>
    Excavation is controlled destruction: every scoop changes the site forever, so the
    whole craft is <strong>recording</strong>. The governing principle is
    <strong>context</strong> — the find's position in the three-dimensional record: which
    layer (<em>stratigraphy</em>), what it lies with (<em>association</em>), how its
    deposit relates to pits, walls, robber-trenches (<em>cross-cutting</em>: a thing cut
    into is older than the cut; the fill is younger than the wall it abuts). Deeper is
    older (<strong>superposition</strong>), layers stack in depositional order, and the
    <strong>Harris matrix</strong> formalises the sequence into a flowchart the whole
    site can share. An artefact with context is evidence; without it, a trophy. This is
    why the antiquities trade is an <em>information</em> crime: a looted pot can be
    dated by style, but everything it knew — its layer, its household, its trade route —
    is unrecoverable. Digging also produces the data types: <strong>artefacts</strong>
    (made things), <strong>ecofacts</strong> (organic remains — seeds, bones, pollen),
    and <strong>features</strong> (non-portable constructions — hearths, postholes,
    floors), each recorded in plan, section, photographs and 3-D coordinates, with
    bulk samples sieved and floated for the tiny (charred grain, fish bones). The
    <strong>typology</strong> — classifying artefacts by form and decoration — does
    double duty as a descriptive system and a <em>relative dating</em> tool: Petrie
    ordered Egyptian pottery into sequences by watching styles evolve (the
    "battleship curve" — a type rare, then common, then rare again), giving
    chronology before any physics existed.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each principle with what it tells you" widget="match" config={anContextMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> deeper is always older. Superposition is the default, and the exceptions are the subject's daily bread: a pit, grave or robber-trench cut down through earlier layers fills with later material, so a Roman coin can sit beneath a Neolithic one without any miracle. The Harris matrix exists precisely because layers interleave — reading a site is logic, not a depth ruler.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> archaeology is about finding beautiful objects. The
  museum case is the by-product; the unit of evidence is the <em>assemblage</em> — the
  pattern of everything from one context. A single gold mask tells less than a
  thousand recorded potsherds, and "treasure" finds without records are mostly
  wreckage of someone else's science.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> excavation is controlled destruction governed by context —
    stratigraphy, superposition with its exceptions, and the Harris matrix that
    formalises the sequence. The next problem begins once the record is in the lab: the
    record is frozen, but the past was moving.
  </p>

  <h2>Analysis: from static remains to dynamic lives</h2>
  <p>
    The record is frozen; the past was moving. <strong>Middle range theory</strong>
    (Binford) supplies the bridge: observe present-day processes that leave deposits —
    knap stone experimentally and study the debitage; follow butchers and mark the
    cut-marks; live with modern foragers (<em>ethnoarchaeology</em>, Binford's Nunamiut
    work) and watch how discard actually works; study how bones decay and scatter
    (<em>taphonomy</em>) — then read the ancient traces against the calibrated
    dictionary. The <strong>interpretive</strong> school (Hodder's post-processualism)
    adds the second bridge: meaning. Symbols are not just adaptations — Çatalhöyük's
    bull horns and plastered skulls, and the burial goods of any era, ask to be read
    as statements, and ethnoarchaeology again supplies the dictionary of plausible
    meanings. And the humble end of the pipeline is the most honest instrument
    archaeology has: <strong>garbage</strong>. Rathje's Tucson Garbage Project
    compared what households said they consumed with what their bins held —
    and found systematic misreporting (alcohol under-reported dramatically) while
    the garbage told the truth. Written sources flatter; middens do not. The
    quantitative side (counts, spatial statistics, sourcing analysis — trace-element
    fingerprints that tie an obsidian blade to its volcano) and the qualitative side
    (typological sequences, use-wear interpretation, iconographic reading) work
    together: the counts find the pattern, the interpretation explains it.
  </p>

  <h2>The dating clocks: each material has its own</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Relative dating</strong> (stratigraphy, typology, seriation) orders things
    without measuring time. <strong>Absolute dating</strong> measures years, and each
    clock has a material and a range. <strong>Radiocarbon</strong>: living things
    exchange carbon with the atmosphere, including radioactive carbon-14; at death
    exchange stops and the isotope decays with a 5,730-year half-life — measurable
    back to roughly 50,000 years, with tree-ring calibration (rings of known age
    correcting the fluctuating atmospheric carbon) making dates calendar-accurate.
    Libby's method won the 1960 Nobel Prize and remains the discipline's backbone.
    <strong>Dendrochronology</strong> counts tree rings against master sequences —
    exact calendar years, regionally, back ~12,000 years, and used to calibrate
    everything else. <strong>Thermoluminescence and OSL</strong> date the last time a
    fired object or buried sediment saw light or heat — clocks for pottery, hearths
    and windblown layers. <strong>Potassium-argon and argon-argon</strong> date
    volcanic rock over millions of years — the clock that anchors the East African
    hominin record by dating the tuffs that sandwich the fossils.
    <strong>Uranium-series</strong> dates cave calcite — the method behind the
    Neanderthal cave-art claims (date the crust over the pigment; the art is older).
    The rule of practice: multiple clocks on one context, cross-checked — a date
    supported by two independent methods is a finding; one method alone is a
    hypothesis.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each dating clock by its material" widget="sort-buckets" config={anClockBuckets} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a radiocarbon date is an exact calendar year. The measurement yields a radiocarbon age that must be calibrated against tree-ring records, because atmospheric carbon-14 has fluctuated through time — so reports give calibrated ranges, often a century or two wide, and "BP" means before 1950, not before today. Precision is a property of the clock plus its calibration, not of the number alone.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="say what a date actually is" widget="match" config={anCalibrationMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> analysis bridges static remains to dynamic lives, and each
    dating clock carries its own material and range — cross-checking is the rule of
    practice. What all of it adds up to is the payoff: reading whole societies from
    bones, buildings and distributions.
  </p>

  <h2>Society from stuff: bones, buildings, distributions</h2>
  <p>
    The payoff sections read the record outward. <strong>Bioarchaeology</strong>: bones
    carry biography — diet (carbon and nitrogen isotopes distinguish C3 and C4
    plant consumption; the maize isotope signature mapped its spread through the
    Americas and can tell a maize farmer's skeleton from a forager's at a glance),
    disease (lesions of leprosy, tuberculosis, treponemal disease), violence
    (embedded projectile points, healed fractures — the Neolithic violence debates
    run on tallies of traumatized skulls), and workload (muscle attachments, joint
    wear — the bioarchaeology of women's labour is rewriting the "man the hunter"
    division-of-labour assumptions). <strong>Buildings encode power</strong>: hall,
    hovel and throne-room floor areas are measurable status; the first palaces
    announce themselves in square metres. <strong>Distribution maps show economies</strong>:
    plot where an artefact type occurs and trade routes, territories and exchange
    systems appear on the map. Together with demography (settlement sizes, cemetery
    age profiles) and palaeoenvironmental archives (pollen cores recording
    deforestation and crop introduction), these strands let archaeology write the
    kind of history writing never captured — of households, women, children, slaves
    and the unspectacular majority.
  </p>

  <h2>Discoveries that rewrote the sequence</h2>
  <p>
    Four case studies show the method producing surprise. <strong>Göbekli Tepe</strong>
    (Turkey, excavated from 1994): monumental T-shaped stone pillars in enclosures,
    radiocarbon-dated from ~9600 BCE — a millennium or more <em>before</em> farming
    appears in the region. Hunter-gatherers built ritual architecture first,
    overturning the assumed sequence (farm → surplus → monuments); the leading
    interpretation now runs the causality backward — the feasts and the building
    labour pulled cultivation into being. <strong>Çatalhöyük</strong> (Turkey,
    ~7500-5700 BCE): a compact town of thousands with no streets — houses entered
    through roofs, burials under floors — and, strikingly, little evidence of
    hierarchy: near-uniform houses, no palace, no elite cemetery. Urbanism without
    a state was possible, and every textbook assumption about what "city" requires
    had to be re-examined. <strong>The Maya LiDAR revolution</strong> (above): the
    lowland civilisation's scale, warfare and sustainability debates all reset —
    and the "Maya collapse" is now widely discussed as transformation, since
    complexity demonstrably re-emerged in the northern lowlands. The
    <strong>Antikythera mechanism</strong> (a shipwreck find, ~100-150 BCE):
    thirty-plus bronze gears computing eclipse calendars — ancient technology was
    not uniformly primitive; the record is a biased sample whose survivors were
    chosen by material durability, not by capability. Each case is the same lesson:
    the sequence of civilisation is an empirical claim, and excavation keeps
    amending it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="bet on the bias in the record" widget="predict" config={anAntikytheraPredict} />
  <Widget no="9" caption="reverse the assumed sequence" widget="order" config={anGobekliOrder} />

  <h2>Ethical issues: whose past is it?</h2>
  <p>
    Archaeology is data collection on other people's ancestors, and the ethics are now
    central, not decorative. <strong>Looting and the antiquities market</strong>
    destroy context at industrial scale (conflict zones turned site-looting into
    revenue — the information crime made explicit); museum acquisitions are now
    audited for provenance. <strong>Repatriation</strong>: the US Native American
    Graves Protection and Repatriation Act (1990) requires returning human remains and
    sacred objects to descendant communities — transforming museum practice — and
    international returns have followed (the Benin bronzes' transfers from European
    and American institutions since 2021). <strong>Human remains and genetics</strong>:
    the Kennewick Man case (a ~8,500-year-old skeleton, contested for two decades,
    resolved when ancient DNA in 2015 showed closer affinity to Native Americans;
    reburied in 2017) is the hinge — science's claim to access yielded to descent
    communities' claim to ancestors, with genetic evidence, ironically, supporting
    the reburial. The professional consensus that has emerged: descendant communities
    are stakeholders in design, interpretation and disposition, not obstacles to it —
    and projects built that way have proved scientifically better too, because more
    knowledge (oral tradition, place names, ritual knowledge) enters the analysis.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why is a looted pot less useful to archaeology than a recorded one, even if both can be dated by style?</li>
    <li>A radiocarbon lab returns a date. Why is it a calibrated range rather than a calendar year?</li>
    <li>What did the Göbekli Tepe discovery overturn, and how?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) the information lives in the context — layer,
    associations, position — and that is exactly what the looter destroys; a stylistic
    date cannot recover the household, trade route or deposit the pot came from. (2)
    because atmospheric carbon-14 has fluctuated through time, the radiocarbon age must be
    calibrated against tree-ring records — so reports give calibrated ranges, and "BP"
    means before 1950, not before today. (3) the assumed sequence farm → surplus →
    monuments: the enclosures date from about 9600 BCE, a millennium or more before
    farming appears in the region, so gathering to build (and feast) may have pulled
    cultivation into being rather than the reverse.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Archaeology studies the human past — the 99% of history that predates writing, plus the unrecorded everyday.</li>
    <li>Context is everything: an artefact with context is evidence; without it, a trophy.</li>
    <li>Stratigraphy reads layers as time, but cuts and fills make exceptions — the Harris matrix exists for them.</li>
    <li>Each dating clock has a material and a range; multiple methods on one context make a finding.</li>
    <li>Middle range theory bridges static remains to dynamic behaviour; garbage tells the truth that written sources flatter.</li>
    <li>Excavation keeps amending the sequence of civilisation — Göbekli Tepe and Çatalhöyük both rewrote it.</li>
    <li>Descendant communities are stakeholders, not obstacles: provenance, repatriation and restraint now govern practice.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate <em>Human Evolution: A Timeline</em> lesson supplies the deep-time
    record this page's clocks date, and <em>The Four Fields</em> shows how archaeology
    triangulates with cultural, biological and linguistic anthropology. For the
    living-societies counterpart — the interviewing this subject cannot do — continue to
    the advanced <em>Ethnographic Methods</em> lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the digging actually happens.</strong> Most excavation today is not
    research but <strong>cultural resource management</strong> — rescue archaeology
    ahead of construction, funded by development. The great urban excavations
    (London's Roman-Medieval layers, Billingsgate, the M25 corridor surveys) are
    CRM products, and the discipline's data explosion comes from them; the research
    agenda increasingly runs on the rescue record. The trade-off — speed and
    salvage against design — is a live professional argument.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Big data and remote everything.</strong> LiDAR (above) is the first wave;
    satellite spectral imaging, drone photogrammetry and open GIS archives are the
    second. Machine learning now classifies pottery sherds and detects cropmarks —
    with the same caveats as any algorithmic classification: the training set
    inherits the sampling biases of a century of excavation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How do you date a layer when the carbon in it is
    old (the "old wood problem" — burning ancient timber resets no clock)? What is
    the honest upper limit of radiocarbon as pre-treatment improves? Can the
    interpretive and processual programmes be reconciled into one method — or is
    their tension itself the discipline's quality control? And what should be
    excavated at all, when the ethics of leaving things in the ground (for better
    techniques later, out of respect now) keep gaining weight? The best fieldwork
    increasingly leaves half the site unexcavated — the archaeology of restraint,
    practising the discipline this page has been teaching: context first, and some
    contexts are best preserved intact.
  </p>
